  "verbalization": "Acrivastine is implicated in the interaction with HepG2 cells through a series of molecular pathways involving lysine-specific demethylase 4D-like. This enzyme is targeted by Acrivastine, and its downstream effectors, such as 5alpha,6alpha-Epoxy(22e,24r)-ergosta-8(14),22-dien-3beta,7alpha-diol and 5-(2-Thienylethynyl)-2-thiophenemethanol, are also targets of Acrivastine. These compounds are ultimately linked to HepG2, suggesting a potential mechanism by which Acrivastine influences cellular processes in this cancer cell line. The evidence highlights a chain of molecular interactions, where Acrivastine acts as a modulator of enzymes that ultimately affect HepG2, potentially through the regulation of gene expression or other cellular functions. The integration of these pathways underscores the role of Acrivastine in modulating cellular responses in HepG2, though the exact mechanism of action remains to be fully elucidated.",
